2026-05-24 01:29:26,614 - INFO - thoughts: ["Here’s how I would approach the feature engineering process to improve model performance by leveraging the dataset attributes and applying relevant operations:\n\n### Step 1: Generate interaction features (ratios and products)\nCombining features can capture interactions between different molecular descriptors. Some interactions, such as ratios or products, might reveal patterns that are not linearly separable using individual features.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: F03[C-O]_ratio_SdO\n# Feature description: Ratio of frequency of C - O at topological distance 3 to sum of dO E-states.\n# Usefulness: This feature can capture the relative abundance of C-O bonds in molecular structures per unit of dO E-states (related to electronic distribution), which may influence biodegradability.\n\ndf['F03[C-O]_ratio_SdO'] = df['F03[C-O]'] / (df['SdO'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: C%_multiply_SpMax_L\n# Feature description: Product of percent Carbon atoms and leading eigenvalue of Laplace matrix.\n# Usefulness: Combining carbon percentage with a spectral descriptor might reveal insights into how carbon structures correlate with biodegradability readiness.\n\ndf['C%_multiply_SpMax_L'] = df['C%'] * df['SpMax_L']\n\n# Feature name: nHDon_plus_nO\n# Feature description: Sum of donor atoms for H-bonds and number of Oxygen atoms.\n# Usefulness: Hydrogen donors (N and O) and oxygen atoms play essential roles in chemical degradation reactions. Their combined influence might correlate stronger with biodegradability.\n\ndf['nHDon_plus_nO'] = df['nHDon'] + df['nO']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Create nonlinear transformations for specific attributes\nApplying mathematical transformations (e.g., logarithms and square roots) to features could capture non-linear relationships and reduce the skewed distributions of some columns.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: log_F04[C-N]\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4.\n# Usefulness: The feature F04[C-N] is highly skewed (range=[0, 36]), and applying log transformation reduces skewness to make patterns more visible for model training.\n\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = np.log1p(df['F04[C-N]'])\n\n# Feature name: sqrt_SDSS_C\n# Feature description: Square root transformation of SdssC (sum of dssC E-states).\n# Usefulness: SdssC has negative values (related to electronic states), making its absolute magnitude useful while reducing the impact of extreme outliers.\n\ndf['sqrt_SDSS_C'] = np.sqrt(np.abs(df['SdssC']))\n\n# Feature name: sigmoid_SpMax_L\n# Feature description: Sigmoid transformation of the leading eigenvalue of the Laplace matrix (SpMax_L).\n# Usefulness: A sigmoid function compresses large values while maintaining interpretability. This might help in identifying thresholds that are relevant for biodegradability.\n\ndf['sigmoid_SpMax_L'] = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-df['SpMax_L']))\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Group-level statistics for categorical-like columns\nEven though the dataset appears numerical, there may still be implicit groups based on certain features like presence/absence of bonds or aromatic groups. Aggregating statistics can improve signal.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: groupbythenmean_B01[C-Br]_associated_BP\n# Feature description: Mean grouping on rows where B01[C-Br] is present for associated Burden Polar attributes (SpPosA_B(p)).\n# Usefulness: Capturing relationships between polarizability and presence of bromine atoms might inform reactivity and biodegradability.\n\nbr_present = df[df['B01[C-Br]'] > 0]\ndf['mean_Br_polar'] = br_present['SpPosA_B(p)'].mean()\n\n# Feature name: is_aromatic_nX_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of aromatic ester count (nArCOOR) and halogen atoms count (nX).\n# Usefulness: Aromatic esters and halogens are indicative of unique molecular pathways that might influence biodegradability differently. This ratio enables exploration.\n\ndf['is_aromatic_nX_ratio'] = df['nArCOOR'] / (df['nX'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: rolling_mean_SM6_B(m)\n# Feature description: Rolling mean to capture trends in spectral moments (Burden matrix weighted by mass).\n# Usefulness: This transformation smoothens out noise from local variations in spectral moments over adjacent rows, potentially finding molecular substructure trends.\n\ndf['rolling_mean_SM6_B(m)'] = df['SM6_B(m)'].rolling(window=5, min_periods=1).mean()\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\nIf some features bear high similarity or appear to have weak correlation with the biodegradability classes, they can be dropped.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column SdssC is dropped\n# SdssC shows almost no variation between values, with Mean = -0.1962 and Std = 0.8008. Other electric state features like average Sanderson electronegativity (Me) may suffice as proxies.\ndf.drop(columns=['SdssC'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\n# Presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 4 exhibits extremely low variability with mean 0.0332 and is mostly zero. This contributes negligible signal to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\n# The number of CRX3 values is consistently 0 for a majority of cases. Its low variability and lack of meaningful differentiation justify its removal.\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Why These Steps Are Useful\n1. **Interaction Features**: Ratios and combined attributes reveal dependencies that align with chemical behavior influencing biodegradability.\n2. **Nonlinear Transformations**: Address non-linear relationships in data while improving feature scaling for downstream models.\n3. **Grouping and Aggregations**: Enables extraction of domain-relevant signals from implicit groups, specifically tied to chemical bond presence/absence.\n\n", "### Possible next three steps for feature engineering:\nBelow are three steps incorporating new features that could potentially improve model performance. The reasoning for each step is provided.\n\n#### Step 1: Interaction feature\n```python\n# `nO_nN_ratio`\n# This column calculates the ratio of the number of oxygen atoms (nO) to the number of nitrogen atoms (nN).\n# Usefulness: Since oxygen and nitrogen atoms are critical in determining the biodegradability of chemicals, \n# their relative proportion might be an important discriminating feature for classification.\ndf['nO_nN_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nN'] + 0.001)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n#### Step 2: Log Transformation for Skewed Features\n```python\n# `log_F03[C-O]`\n# This column applies log transformation to the `F03[C-O]` feature to reduce the impact of extreme values.\n# Usefulness: The distribution of `F03[C-O]` is highly skewed, as evidenced by its large range relative to its quartiles. \n# Log transformation reduces skewness and improves model performance when handling exponential-like feature distributions.\nimport numpy as np\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log(df['F03[C-O]'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to handle zero values during log transformation.\n```end\n\n#### Step 3: Z-score Normalization for Distribution Centering\n```python\n# `zscore_SM6_L`\n# This column applies z-score normalization to the `SM6_L` (Spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) feature.\n# Usefulness: Since `SM6_L` has a wide range of values with a noticeable spread, normalizing the feature focuses on \n# relative positions (as the number of heavy atoms or circuit values could influence biodegradability).\ndf['zscore_SM6_L'] = (df['SM6_L'] - df['SM6_L'].mean()) / df['SM6_L'].std()\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping columns\nAs part of cleansing, removing columns that may have limited impact on the downstream classification task based on exploratory data analysis is suggested.\n\n#### Step 1: Drop `B04[C-Br]`\n```python\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\n# The presence/absence of `C-Br` at topological distance 4 is always zero for most instances (Q1, Median, Q3 = 0.0). \n# Its mean and tight standard deviation don't provide discriminative information for classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n#### Step 2: Drop `N-073`\n```python\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# The feature `N-073` (Ar2NH / Ar3N / Ar2N-Al / R..N..R) has extremely low variance (mean ~0.0253, Std ~0.1932) \n# and appears to contribute minimal information since all quartiles are zero.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n#### Step 3: Drop `nCRX3`\n```python\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\n# The feature `nCRX3` (number of CRX3) also has minimal variation (Q1, Median, Q3 = 0). \n# Features with very low variance tend to provide little signal for supervised learning tasks.\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\nBy performing the above feature engineering steps (new and dropped features), the dataset becomes tuned to emphasize features that contribute to classification while removing redundant or less impactful ones. These engineered transformations should strengthen model predictions and reduce irrelevant noise."]
