# Step 1: Generate interaction features between C% and SdO
# Feature name: Interaction_C_SdO
# Feature description: Multiply the percentage of carbon atoms (C%) with the sum of dO E-states (SdO).
# Usefulness: The interaction feature may capture the combined influence of carbon atom concentration and the E-states of oxygen in predicting biodegradability. Higher carbon percentages and SdO levels can collectively influence the chemical's readiness for biodegradation.
df['Interaction_C_SdO'] = df['C%'] * df['SdO']
end

python
# Step 2: Apply z-score normalization to highly variable columns
# Feature name: SdO_zscore
# Feature description: Standardize the sum of dO E-states (SdO) using z-score normalization.
# Usefulness: Columns like SdO have high variance (Std=12.04). Normalizing them can help stabilize the influence of outliers or high variability on machine learning algorithms. Standardization can make features comparable and improve model performance.
df['SdO_zscore'] = (df['SdO'] - df['SdO'].mean()) / df['SdO'].std()
end

python
# Step 3: Compute logarithmic transformation for Reciprocal_LOC
# Feature name: Log_Reciprocal_LOC
# Feature description: Apply a logarithmic transformation to Reciprocal_LOC to reduce skewness and bring extremely large values closer to the range of other features.
# Usefulness: Reciprocal_LOC has an extremely wide range with a mean of ~60 million, indicating potential skewness. Transforming this feature can make it more comparable to others and prevent domination of model learning due to its large scale.
df['Log_Reciprocal_LOC'] = np.log1p(df['Reciprocal_LOC'])
end

