 transformations allow the model to discover more complex patterns.\n\npython\n# Log_SpMax_L\n# Logarithm of the leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix (SpMax_L). \n# Usefulness: Eigenvalues often scale exponentially, and using log transformation makes them comparable, reducing variance which can aid the model.\ndf['Log_SpMax_L'] = np.log(df['SpMax_L'] + 1e-9)  # Avoid log(0).\n\n# Sqrt_SM6_L\n# Square root of the spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix (SM6_L). \n# Usefulness: Spectral moment-based features may have nonlinear impacts, and square root transformations can make large values more interpretable in downstream tasks.\ndf['Sqrt_SM6_L'] = np.sqrt(df['SM6_L'])\n\n# Reciprocal_Loc\n# Reciprocal of Lopping centric index (LOC). \n# Usefulness: Smaller LOC values are more relevant for certain molecular structures associated with better biodegradability. Reciprocal scaling emphasizes lower values.\ndf['Reciprocal_LOC'] = 1 / (df['LOC'] + 1e-9)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Generate statistical aggregates for features known to represent similar chemical properties\nBy grouping features with similar chemical properties (e.g., frequencies of bonds or specific atoms), it’s possible to create aggregated statistics (mean, max, etc.) that capture important aspects of chemical composition that correlate with biodegradability.\n\npython\n# Average_C_N_Frequency\n# Average frequency of C-N bonds across different topological distances ([F02[C-N], F03[C-N], F04[C-N]]).\n# Usefulness: C-N bonds at different topological distances can impact biodegradability; aggregating this information provides a holistic measure of C-N frequency.\ndf['Average_C_N_Frequency'] = df[['F02[C-N]', 'F03[C-N]', 'F04[C-N]']].mean(axis=1)\n\n# Max_C_N_Frequency \n# Maximum frequency value among C-N topological distances.\n# Usefulness: Highlighting molecules with extreme C-N frequencies may help the model identify edge cases related to specific chemical bonds.\ndf['Max_C_N_Frequency'] = df[['F02[C-N]', 'F03[C-N]', 'F04[C-N]']].max(axis=1)\n\n# Heavy_atom_sum\n# Sum of heavy atoms (nHM) and number of terminal primary C(sp3) (nCp). \n# Usefulness: Total heavy atom count, including terminal carbons, provides a broader view of molecular complexity associated with biodegradability.\ndf['Heavy_atom_sum'] = df['nHM'] + df['nCp']\nend\n\n### Dropping Columns to Reduce Noise\n\nSome descriptors have very low variance, extremely skewed distributions, or limited predictive power based on exploratory analysis. These features can be dropped to improve downstream model efficiency.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped\n# Reason: Frequency of N-N at topological distance 1 (F01[N-N]) has a very low mean (0.0363) and standard deviation (0.2457). Most values are 0 (high sparsity), making it uninformative for biodegradability prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nArCOOR is dropped\n# Reason: Number of esters (aromatic) (nArCOOR) has very low values (Mean = 0.0585, Std = 0.3441), with almost all entries being 0 (high sparsity). It is unlikely to contribute strongly to classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['nArCOOR'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\n# Reason: Presence/absence of C - Br at topological distance 4 (B04[C-Br]) is binary and has an extremely low mean (0.0332), meaning it is almost always absent in the dataset, providing no significant discrimination power.\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Summary\n1. Interaction Features: Adding ratios like C_O_Ratio, N_HDon_per_mass, and Halogen_to_C_ratio captures real-world relationships between features.\n2. Non-linear Transformations: Log, square root, and reciprocal transformations help handle wide ranges or skewed values of features, better modeling nonlinear impacts.\n3. Statistical Aggregates: Aggregating related features provides macro-level insights into the chemical composition.\n\nThese steps aim to extract higher-level patterns, focusing on chemical and molecular properties relevant to biodegradability. Dropping irrelevant and sparse columns removes noise, improving the model's focus on impactful signals.", "python\n# Feature 1: Log_SpMax_L_plus_Me\n# Feature description: Sum of 'Log_SpMax_L' (logarithm of leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix) and 'Me' (mean atomic Sanderson electronegativity).\n# Usefulness: The sum may capture the combined relationship between molecular connectivity and electronegativity, which could better characterize chemicals in terms of their biodegradability potential.\n\ndf['Log_SpMax_L_plus_Me'] = df['Log_SpMax_L'] + df['Me']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: SdO_zscore\n# Feature description: Z-Score standardization of the 'SdO' feature (sum of dO E-states).\n# Usefulness: Transforming 'SdO' to a Z-Score normalizes the distribution, which is skewed (based on the presence of zero-heavy data). This ensures fair contribution during model training and could highlight the relative strength of this feature, especially for non-zero entries.\n\nfrom scipy.stats import zscore\ndf['SdO_zscore'] = zscore(df['SdO'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: SpMax_L_Log_SpMax_L_ratio\n# Feature description: The ratio of 'SpMax_L' (leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix) to 'Log_SpMax_L' (logarithm of leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: This ratio might reveal nonlinear and scale-dependent relationships between 'SpMax_L' and 'Log_SpMax_L'. It could capture structural molecular characteristics relevant to distinguishing ready and not ready biodegradable chemicals.\n\ndf['SpMax_L_Log_SpMax_L_ratio'] = df['SpMax_L'] / (df['Log_SpMax_L'] + 1e-8)  # Add small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Reciprocal_LOC is dropped\n# The 'Reciprocal_LOC' feature has extreme values, evidenced by a range reaching up to 10^9 and a mean of over 60 million. This could introduce numerical instability and dominate the model, potentially leading to worse performance.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Reciprocal_LOC'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column C% is dropped\n# The 'C%' feature (percentage of Carbon atoms) may provide redundant information, as other features like 'nCp' (number of primary C(sp3)) or 'Heavy_atom_sum' may already capture the structural contribution of Carbon atoms in a more granular and meaningful way.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['C%'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column NssssC is dropped\n# The 'NssssC' feature (number of atoms of type ssssC) has extremely low variability, with a zero median and a mean close to zero. It is unlikely to contribute significantly to the model's ability to differentiate classes.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['NssssC'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
